Parentheses and `as`/`satisfies` around a default export or a variable's value don't change what it is: a wrapped identifier, class, or arrow function prints as if unwrapped. An array with only holes isn't empty; one with only a comment is.
== args
input/wrapped.ts
== file input/wrapped.ts
export default (App);
export default App as Component;
export default (class Store {});
export default [,];
export default [/* none */];
export default {
  // none
};
const double = ((x: number) => x * 2);
